content:
E27 shows all 16 retry violations come from a single supplier "Red Sea Parts" — strongly pointing to bad_input (changed invoice layout from that supplier). DB sessions (E28) are all normal (1 each), ruling out connection exhaustion as the cause of the broad slowdown.

Now I need to understand the broad throughput drop at t=560 (E3, E4, E5, E8, E9). With DB connections normal, the remaining suspects are:
- The 16 "Red Sea Parts" messages consuming retry budget and clogging the queue (E10 shows queue depth 113), starving legitimate work
- Something else causing the simultaneous drop across invoice-agent, worker, payment-service, sync-job at t=560

The queue depth of 113 building since t=530 (E10) with processing at 0.3/s vs 2.3/s peak (M1) is consistent with the queue being flooded by retries (E6 shows 7.65/s retry rate). Let me check storage and whether the queue backlog explains all downstream drops.

```json
{
  "investigate": [
    {
      "tool": "sql",
      "query": "SELECT service, used_mb, quota_mb FROM storage ORDER BY (used_mb * 1.0 / quota_mb) DESC",
      "why": "Rule out storage exhaustion as a cause of the broad multi-service slowdown at t=560, since DB connections are normal"
    },
    {
      "tool": "sql",
      "query": "SELECT state, COUNT(*) as cnt, AVG(attempts) as avg_attempts, MAX(attempts) as max_attempts FROM messages GROUP BY state",
      "why": "Understand queue composition — how many messages are exhausted/dead vs pending, to confirm retry flood is starving legitimate processing"
    }
  ],
  "hypotheses": [],
  "summary": "Checking storage exhaustion and queue message state distribution to confirm whether the retry flood from Red Sea Parts bad_input fully explains the broad throughput drop."
}
```
